{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "353",
    "case_summary": "A young girl initially came under care at age 4 years. At age 6, she developed an irregular brown-black patch on her left lateral ankle, measuring 3.6 × 3 cm triangular hyperpigmented, asymptomatic patch consisting of confluent mottled black and brown macules and several satellite lesions. At age 9, parents noted slow but progressive growth of the hyperpigmented patch, which had increased to 5.5 × 5-cm. At age 12, two discrete areas of regression were noted.\n\nSeveral brown macules coalescing into a patch with surrounding satellite lesions on lateral ankle. On dermoscopy, discontiguous and irregular homogenous blue, brown, and gray areas with gray-brown structureless areas and a negative pigment network were observed. No induration was palpated except at the previous biopsy site.\n\n- Histopathology (First biopsy at age 9):  Shows a compound nevus with large nested melanocytes and bridging at the dermal-epidermal junction consistent with an atypical compound nevus.    - Histopathology (Second biopsy at age 12):  Shows atypical nevus with areas of dermal fibrosis.    - Gene Expression Testing:  Test for 23 melanoma genes performed (Myriad myPath)  Score: -7.1 (where scores from -16.7 to -2.1 are consistent with a benign nevus)    - Images:    Image 1 Title: Epidermolysis Bullosa Nevus at 9 Years of Age    Image 1A Description: A large, dark brown patch consisting of coalescing macules and satellite lesions    Image 1B Description: Compound melanocytic proliferation with appropriate maturation, random atypia with large nested cells characterized by prominent nucleoli and heavy pigmentation        Image 2 Title: Epidermolysis Bullosa Nevus at 12 Years of Age    Image 2A Description: The patch shows areas of regression on the medial and left upper borders    Image 2B Description: Compound melanocytic proliferation marked by random nuclear atypia and broad areas of regressive fibrosis",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Unilateral Ankle Pigmentation with Atypical Nevus Features",
